## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of dynamic connectivity under incremental edge insertions and practical amortized analysis in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts

- Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression and union by size
- Amortized α(n) (inverse Ackermann function) for near-constant operations
- Batch processing efficiency vs full recomputation approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.components = n;
  }

  find(a) {
    if (this.parent[a] !== a) {
      this.parent[a] = this.find(this.parent[a]);
    }
    return this.parent[a];
  }

  union(a, b) {
    const rootA = this.find(a);
    const rootB = this.find(b);
    if (rootA === rootB) return false;

    if (this.size[rootA] < this.size[rootB]) {
      [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
    }
    this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
    this.size[rootA] += this.size[rootB];
    this.components--;
    return true;
  }
}

function processBatches(n, batches) {
  const dsu = new DSU(n);
  const results = [];

  for (const batch of batches) {
    for (const [u, v] of batch) {
      dsu.union(u, v);
    }
    results.push(dsu.components);
  }

  return results;
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing:** O(n) time to initialize DSU arrays, O(n) memory for parent and size arrays.

**Per-batch updates:** O(B·α(n)) time for B union operations with path compression and union by size.

**Total complexity:** O(n + m·α(n)) time, O(n) memory.

**Comparison to recomputation:** Full DFS/BFS after each batch would cost O(n + m) per batch, totaling O((n + m)·(m/B)) time—significantly worse for large graphs with many small batches.

The DSU approach leverages amortized near-constant operations, making it ideal for dynamic connectivity scenarios with incremental updates.

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical dynamic approximate all-pairs shortest paths (APSP) with shared structure; tests understanding of trade-offs between update costs and query time in batch-dynamic settings.

## Key Concepts
- Batch-dynamic graphs with nonnegative edge weights
- Hub-based distance sketches for approximate APSP
- Amortized analysis across multiple query pairs
- Trade-offs between approximation accuracy ε and computational efficiency
- Incremental shortest path algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hub-based distance approximation: dist(u,v) ≈ min_h∈H [dist(u,h) + dist(h,v)]

class HubBasedAPSP {
  constructor(graph, epsilon) {
    this.k = Math.ceil((1/epsilon) * Math.log(graph.n));
    this.hubs = this.selectHubs(graph);
    this.distances = new Map(); // hub -> vertex distances
  }

  updateBatch(edges) {
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      this.distances.set(hub, this.incrementalDijkstra(hub, edges));
    }
  }

  query(u, v) {
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      const dist = this.distances.get(hub).get(u) + 
                   this.distances.get(hub).get(v);
      minDist = Math.min(minDist, dist);
    }
    return minDist;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of batch processing with dynamic statistics and tight performance guarantees, blending order-statistics trees with buffering for amortization analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics trees or multisets with subtree sizes and prefix sums
- Bulk-merge and batch amortization techniques
- Maintaining running sums to compute L1 deviations efficiently

## Code Example
```javascript
class TwoTierMedian {
  constructor() {
    this.committed = new OrderStatisticsTree();
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  insertBatch(batch) {
    this.buffer.push(...batch);
    this.buffer.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    this.committed.bulkMerge(this.buffer);
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  getMedian() {
    return this.committed.findByRank(Math.floor(this.committed.size / 2));
  }

  getSumAbsoluteDeviations() {
    const median = this.getMedian();
    return this.committed.sumAbsoluteDeviations(median);
  }
}
```

## Bulk-Merge and Rebalancing Strategy
Sort the buffer, then merge two sorted sequences into the OST using an in-order traversal. After merging, rebalance the tree (e.g., via treap randomization or AVL-style rotations) to maintain O(log n) operations.

## Amortized Per-Element Cost
Let B be the batch size. Sorting takes O(B log B), bulk-merge takes O(B), and rebalancing adds O(B). Amortized per element: O(log B) per batch element, which is optimal compared to O(log n) per individual insert.

## Memory Bounds
- OST stores all committed elements: O(n)
- Buffer stores one batch: O(B)
- Prefix sums at each node: O(n) additional memory

## Two Optimizations
1. **Lazy Buffering**: Accumulate multiple batches before merging to reduce merge frequency
2. **Hybrid Selection**: Use quickselect on the buffer first to find median bounds before querying the OST

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests offline reasoning for dynamic bipartiteness under batched updates, along with the ability to combine DSU with rollback and time-block decomposition.

## Key Concepts
- **Parity (Bipartiteness) DSU**: Maintains connected components with parity labels to detect odd cycles, enabling O(α(N)) union-find operations.
- **Rollback / Persistent State**: Allows reverting DSU state within a block to test multiple edge configurations efficiently.
- **Time-based Block Decomposition**: Divides updates into blocks, rebuilding the DSU at block boundaries while processing updates within blocks using rollback.
- **Trade-offs**: Balancing rebuild cost (at block boundaries) against per-update cost (within blocks).

## Impact of Batch Size B
- **Larger B**: More edges processed per batch → fewer rebuilds → lower amortized cost per edge, but higher memory overhead for rollback structures.
- **Smaller B**: More frequent rebuilds → higher amortized cost, but lower memory since fewer rollback checkpoints are needed.
- **Optimal B**: Typically Θ(√M) balances O(√M · α(N)) per batch with O(M) space.

## Code Example
```cpp
struct ParityDSU {
    vector<int> parent, rank, parity;
    struct Change { int u, oldParent, oldRank, oldParity; };
    vector<Change> stack;

    ParityDSU(int n) : parent(n), rank(n, 0), parity(n, 0) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        return find(parent[x]);
    }

    int findWithParity(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        int p = findWithParity(parent[x]);
        parity[x] ^= parity[parent[x]];
        return parent[x] = p;
    }

    bool unite(int x, int y) {
        x = findWithParity(x);
        y = findWithParity(y);
        if (x == y) return parity[x] != parity[y];
        if (rank[x] < rank[y]) swap(x, y);
        stack.push_back({y, parent[y], rank[y], parity[y]});
        parent[y] = x;
        if (rank[x] == rank[y]) rank[x]++;
        parity[y] = parity[x] ^ parity[y] ^ 1;
        return true;
    }

    void snapshot() { stack.clear(); }
    void rollback() {
        while (!stack.empty()) {
            auto c = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();
            parent[c.u] = c.oldParent;
            rank[c.u] = c.oldRank;
            parity[c.u] = c.oldParity;
        }
    }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental counting, batch updates, and tight per-event amortized bounds, plus modulo-based pair counting with constant extra space.

## Key Concepts
- Residue class bookkeeping with O(1) per update
- Pair counting with modulo constraints
- Amortized vs worst-case trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// insert/update logic
function apply(x, D, freq, total, op){
  const r = x % D;
  const idx = (D - r) % D;
  if(op==='insert'){ total += freq[idx]; freq[r]++; }
  else { freq[r]--; total -= freq[idx]; }
  return total;
}
```

